Abby Medallon, Kurt Colstead, Delaney Hall, and Katie Mingle. And Roman Mars is the true fantasy of this whole series. In the first half of the 20th century, French high fashion was incredibly lucrative, but it's not as though Paris was making all that profit from the couple thousand women who could afford looks fresh off the runway. The big money maker was that department stores all over the world copied these looks and paid money to copy them. There was actually something to refer to as a licensed copy.
艾比·梅达隆（Abby Medallon）、库尔特·科尔斯特德（Kurt Colstead）、德兰尼·霍尔（Delaney Hall）和凯蒂·明格尔（Katie Mingle）。而罗曼·马尔斯（Roman Mars）则是整个系列真正的幻想。在20世纪上半叶，法国高级时装极其赚钱，但并不是说巴黎的所有利润都来自那几千个买得起刚下T台的新款服装的女性。真正的大金主是，世界各地的百货公司都在抄袭这些款式，并付费购买抄袭权。实际上有一种东西被称为授权复制品。

75
00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:47.000
So that was if you were a French couturier, you could sell a license to a design. Retailers like barnies or orbox or wool warts, big stores all over the world would buy sketches or patterns from French designers and recreate the outfits to sell in their shops. And the label would say like, do you buy Bergerff's? So there's various labels.
所以这就是，如果你是一个法国高级时装设计师，你可以出售设计的许可证。巴尼百货（Barneys）、博克思（Orbox）或伍尔沃斯（Woolworths）等零售商，世界各地的许多大商店都会从法国设计师那里购买草图或纸样，并在他们的店铺里重新制作服装进行销售。标签上会写着，比如，你买的是伯格多夫的吗？所以有各种各样的标签。

76
00:38:47.000 --> 00:39:07.000
Ariel Alaya curated an exhibit about counterfeits at the Fashion Institute of Technology. And she showed me a label from one of these licensed copies. So in here it says, Bergerff Goodman on the Plaza, New York, and it has the customer's name, the date, which is 1949, and the serial number to this, because they were only able to do a certain amount of copies
阿里尔·阿莱亚（Ariel Alaya）在纽约时装学院（Fashion Institute of Technology）策划了一个关于假冒商品的展览。她向我展示了其中一件授权复制品的标签。所以这里写着，纽约广场上的伯格多夫·古德曼（Bergdorf Goodman），上面有顾客的姓名、日期（即1949年）以及序列号，因为他们只能制作一定数量的复制品。

77
00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:32.000
that was in the licensing agreement. These licensed copies were nowhere near as expensive and rare as the original runway designs, but they were still to a lesser degree, expensive and rare. So a Couture like Dior, he made sure that there was only maybe five or ten of that piece reproduced, because you want to maintain the exclusivity. This system of licensed copies more or less ended by the 1970s.
这包含在许可协议中。这些授权复制品远没有原本的T台设计那么昂贵和稀有，但它们在较小程度上依然是昂贵且稀有的。因此，像迪奥（Dior）这样的大师，他会确保某个款式只被复制生产五到十件，因为你要保持排他性。这种授权复制品的体系在20世纪70年代前后基本结束了。

78
00:39:32.000 --> 00:39:37.000
People didn't really care about wearing an entire designer outfit anymore, but they did still care about the clout of designers. You all of a sudden have it switched to more accessories. You have handbags and things like that. If you look at the 1980s with Logomania, that's where you start to see all of the monograms on the bags. Your next articles of interest are knockoffs. There aren't comments yet for this episode. Click on any sentence in the transcript to leave a comment.
